course: biology
lesson: 188
title: Record-breakers: the most venomous and poisonous
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Venomous and poisonous are not the same: a venomous animal pushes its venom into you with a bite or sting, like a snake or a scorpion, while a poisonous one is harmful if you touch or eat it, like a poison frog or a pufferfish.
explain: A simple way to remember: if it bites you and you get hurt, it is venomous; if you bite it and you get hurt, it is poisonous.
explain: Some of the most dangerous are small and pretty: a tiny golden frog, a cone shell lying on the sand, a bird with poison in its feathers, so the safest rule is to look but never pick up a wild animal.
explain: Venom also saves lives: snake venom is collected to make antivenom, and most scorpions and spiders are no real danger to people at all.
fact: The golden poison frog is one of the most poisonous animals, with enough poison in its skin to kill several people, and it gets the poison from insects it eats in the wild.
fact: Snake venom is collected to make antivenom: tiny amounts are given to horses or sheep, whose blood makes antibodies (helpers that fight the venom), and antivenom saves thousands of lives every year.
fact: Scorpions glow blue-green under ultraviolet light because of a chemical in their outer layer; scientists are not sure why, but they use UV lamps to find scorpions at night.
fact: The deathstalker is one of the most venomous scorpions, and its venom can be dangerous to children, but most scorpions are not dangerous to people.
fact: The Brazilian wandering spider is one of the most venomous spiders; it wanders at night instead of making webs and sometimes hides in bananas, but bites are rare and an antivenom exists.
fact: The hooded pitohui of New Guinea is one of the few poisonous birds, with poison in its feathers and skin that it gets from beetles it eats.
fact: The geography cone snail stings fish with a harpoon-like tooth and has one of the deadliest venoms, so people should never pick up cone shells.
fact: The story that daddy-long-legs are the most venomous but cannot bite is a myth: two kinds of animal get that name, and harvestmen have no venom at all, while cellar spiders have only weak venom.
fact: Pufferfish, called fugu in Japan, have poison in the liver and other organs, so only trained chefs may prepare them, and one fish can hold enough to kill about thirty people.
fact: Stingrays lie buried in sand and stab with their tail spine only when pinned or trodden on, so shuffling your feet in warm shallow sea warns them to swim away.
fact: Honeybees die after stinging people because the barbed stinger sticks in skin and tears out, but not when stinging other insects.
exercise: What is the difference between venomous and poisonous? | Venomous animals bite or sting; poisonous ones harm you if touched or eaten | They mean exactly the same | Poisonous animals bite; venomous ones are harmful to eat | Only plants can be poisonous
exercise: Where does the golden poison frog get its poison in the wild? | From the insects it eats | From the water it swims in | It makes it from sunlight | From the leaves it sits on
exercise: How is snake venom used to make antivenom? | Tiny amounts are given to horses or sheep, whose blood makes antibodies | It is mixed with sugar | It is boiled for hours until it is safe, then put into small bottles for doctors | It is frozen and melted
exercise: Why do scientists shine UV lamps at night in the desert? | Scorpions glow blue-green under UV light | To keep the snakes warm in the cold desert nights | To scare away bats | To grow cactus
exercise: Are most scorpions dangerous to people? | No, most are not, though the deathstalker can be | Yes, all of them are deadly, even the tiny pale ones | Only baby scorpions are | Scorpions have no venom
exercise: Why might a Brazilian wandering spider turn up in bananas? | It wanders at night instead of making webs and hides there | It eats bananas | It lays its eggs only inside ripe fruit, so the bunches carry them | Farmers put it there
exercise: Which bird has poison in its feathers? | The hooded pitohui of New Guinea | The robin that sings in English gardens | The penguin | The parrot
exercise: Where does the pitohui get its poison? | From beetles it eats | From the sea | From flowers | It makes it from water
exercise: How does a geography cone snail catch fish? | It stings them with a harpoon-like tooth | It chases them fast | It squeezes them | It traps them in a web of sticky slime on the reef
exercise: What should you do if you find a cone shell on a beach? | Never pick it up | Hold it to your ear | Take it home in a pocket | Turn it over with your fingers
exercise: Is it true that daddy-long-legs are the most venomous but cannot bite? | No, it is a myth: harvestmen have no venom and cellar spiders weak venom | Yes, it is proven | Yes, but only at night | Yes, they are deadlier than snakes, but their fangs are too small to bite people
exercise: Why may only trained chefs prepare pufferfish? | Its liver and other organs hold a deadly poison | It bites the chef | It explodes in the pan when it is cooked too long | It is too spiky to touch
exercise: Why do people shuffle their feet in warm shallow sea? | To warn buried stingrays so they swim away | To keep warm | To find shells | To chase away the crabs that nip at your toes
exercise: When does a stingray use its tail spine? | Only when it is pinned or trodden on | Whenever it sees a person in the water | To catch fish | To dig in the sand
exercise: Why does a honeybee die after stinging a person? | Its barbed stinger sticks in the skin and tears out | It runs out of air because it flies too fast after stinging | It eats the venom | It gets too angry
exercise: Does a honeybee die when it stings another insect? | No, the stinger does not get stuck | Yes, always, whatever it stings, bee or wasp | Only on Sundays | Bees never sting insects
test: «it bit me, so it's poisonous right?» What is right? | If it bites or stings you, it is venomous | Right, biting means it is poisonous to eat | Biting means harmless | Only frogs bite
test: «spotted a scorpion: 8 legs, 2 claws and 1 tail, mate found 3 of them, how many legs» | 24 | 33 | 9 | 16
test: «2 wandering spiders n a bee, how many legs» | 22 | 20 | 18 | 24
test: «daddy longlegs is the deadliest spider but its fangs are too short, my teacher said» What is right? | That is a myth; harvestmen have no venom and cellar spiders weak venom | Right, its fangs are too short | Right, it kills more people than snakes | It has no legs
test: «golden frog in the zoo, can i hold it? it's tiny» What should you do? | Do not touch it; wild golden frogs are among the most poisonous animals | Yes, tiny frogs are always safe | Yes, lick it to check | Hold it with wet hands
test: In The Jungle Book, the snake Kaa only hypnotises Mowgli. What do real scientists do with snake venom? | Collect it to make antivenom that saves lives | Use it to hypnotise people, like the snake in the films | Pour it on plants | Nothing, venom is useless
test: What would happen if you stepped hard on a stingray hiding in the sand? | It might stab you with its tail spine in defence | It would chase you | It would explode | It would bite you with its sharp teeth, like a shark
test: «bees die every time they sting» What is right? | Honeybees die after stinging people, but not after stinging insects | Right, every time | Bees never die from stinging | Only wasps die after stinging; bees can sting people again and again
test: «found a pretty cone shell, gonna pop it in my pocket» What should you do? | Leave it: the snail inside can sting with deadly venom | Pocket it, because empty shells are always safe to keep | Hold it to your ear | Squeeze it to check
test: «zoo poison frogs aren't poisonous? sounds fake» Why might that be true? | Wild ones get their poison from insects, which zoo frogs do not eat | Zoo frogs are plastic | Zoo keepers wash them every day, and the soap takes all the poison off | Zoo frogs are always babies
test: «why glow a scorpion with a lamp, scorpion disco?» | Scientists use UV lamps to find scorpions at night | To make them dance | To warm them up | To change their colour forever, so that they look prettier
exam: Identify the distinction between venomous and poisonous animals. | Venomous animals inject toxin by bite or sting, whereas poisonous animals are harmful when touched or consumed | The terms are entirely synonymous | Poisonous animals inject toxin, whereas venomous animals are harmful when consumed | Venomous animals are invariably larger
exam: Ascertain the means by which antivenom is produced. | Minute quantities of venom are administered to horses or sheep, whose blood produces antibodies | Venom is boiled until rendered harmless | Venom is combined with sugar and dried | It is extracted directly from the leaves of plants
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the popular account of the daddy-long-legs? | It is a myth, harvestmen possessing no venom and cellar spiders only weak venom | It is established scientific fact | It is true of harvestmen only | It is true of all spiders without exception
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the honeybee's sting. | The bee perishes after stinging a person, its barbed stinger becoming lodged in the skin | The bee perishes after stinging any creature whatsoever | The bee never perishes after stinging | The bee possesses no stinger
